From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bpereira <bpereira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 15:10:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Evict the shaped text of inline roots far from the viewport

Shaped runs, glyphs and lines are the largest derived data of a long
document, yet once a root's box is settled only paint and geometry
queries read them. After each resolve, inline roots more than two
viewport heights away drop their shaped layout. TextLayout::layout is
the only way to read it and rebuilds it on demand from the DOM, the
saved inline box sizes and indent, and the last line break.

- Inline roots are shaped inside their layout call, and their text is
  filled there instead of during box construction, so an evicted root
  keeps no placeholder text or lines.
- An evicted root keeps a compact break summary (48 bytes) so layout can
  rebreak and measure it, read its line extents, and lay out its
  absolutely positioned boxes, pre-wrap text and top/bottom aligned
  spans without reshaping; the summary is dropped once the root is
  rebuilt. Roots without a summary, and lines shaped around floats, stay
  shaped.
- Recently laid out roots, roots near the viewport or inside a caller's
  extent, the last 256 roots a pass broke, and roots read since the last
  scan stay shaped, so per-frame readers do not reshape every frame.
  Inner scrolling rescans, roots a measure broke are evicted as after a
  full layout, a viewport with no height keeps everything resident, and
  a content slot that differs only by rounding counts as unchanged.
- A root's shaped layout and text share one allocation, its text
  classification is one byte, and the rebuild context lives on the tree.
- Element clones discard derived text layouts.

Text eviction is on by default. Layout, geometry and paint output are
unchanged.

Signed-off-by: bpereira <bpereira@cloudflare.com>
